New York's Attorney General is launching  an investigation after uber admitted it  failed to disclose a massive data breach  for more than a year the ride-sharing  company said yesterday that hackers  stole the personal data of more than 57  million customers and drivers that  includes names email addresses and phone  numbers Carter Evans is in Los Angeles  with how uber handled the breach Carter  good morning good morning you may be  wondering why I took over a year to  disclose the stolen data which it was  legally required to report well the  company's new CEO had the same question  and decided to come clean after recently  learning about the breach which the  company's previous CEO reportedly kept  under wraps  uber decided to steer clear of federal  regulators when it learned to the  widespread hack in 2016 instead of  reporting the incident the  multibillion-dollar company chose to pay  hackers $100,000 to delete the stolen  data it's not known whether the hackers  followed through technology reporter  eric newcomer broke the story for  bloomberg the crazy thing is definitely  that uber didn't tell anyone for more  than a year their belief was okay we'll  pay them they'll delete it everything  will be fine  along with the names email addresses and  phone numbers hackers also obtained  around six hundred thousand license  numbers from uber drivers in a statement  uber says it's outside forensics experts  have not seen any indication the trip  location history credit card numbers  bank account numbers social security  numbers or dates of birth were  downloaded this incident is going to  give any sort of reasonable person pause  about how much they can trust the  company at the time of the hack goober  was already under investigation for  privacy violations while dealing with  the troubled tenure of embattled CEO  Travis kalanick in a blog post Tuesday  Uber's new CEO Derek ozra Shahi wrote  none of this should have happened and I  will not make excuses for it we are  changing the way we do business  darah the new CEO is trying to clean  things up but it's going to take time he  needs to recruit new leaders and really  change the culture and  that's a big project now uber has fired  a security executive and one other  executive we reached out to former uber  CEO Travis kalanick but have not heard  back  Gale alright thank you very much Carter
 

